\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) generalize well semantically, but VLMs are pre-trained mainly on high-level understanding tasks such as VQA and therefore under-emphasize low-level visual and spatial cues and physical dynamics that precise manipulation needs. Conversely, prediction-oriented pre-training, such as future frame generation, improves spatial grounding but lacks semantic robustness. We propose UP-VLA, a unified autoregressive VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) on image-text data and future visual prediction (PRE) on robot video and action data in a single Phi-1.5 backbone. Unified prompting and tailored attention masks mix continuous MMU image tokens and discrete predictive tokens, and a joint prediction-and-understanding mechanism conditions action generation on both a generated scene description and predicted future image tokens. UP-VLA reaches an average completed length of 4.08 on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, a 33\% improvement over GR-1 (3.06), and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D (GR-1: 4.21). Real-world Franka Emika Panda experiments show gains in both semantic generalization to unseen objects and precise manipulation, and ablations show that both prediction and understanding are needed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models increasingly leverage large vision-language models (VLMs) to improve open-world generalization in robotics \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla,black2024pi_0,li2023vision}. VLMs offer strong semantic grounding and reasoning, but they are predominantly pre-trained on high-level understanding tasks such as visual question answering, and tend to under-emphasize low-level visual and spatial cues (depth, size, relative pose) and physical dynamics \citep{chen2024spatialvlm}. These omissions matter for precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes.

Prior work shows a dichotomy (\cref{fig:intro,fig:compare}). VLM-initialized VLAs excel at semantic generalization but struggle with fine-grained control. Prediction-oriented pre-training, such as future frame or video generation \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero,du2024learning}, enhances spatial grounding and physical consistency but lacks semantic robustness. This gap motivates a unified paradigm that keeps both high-level multi-modal understanding and low-level future prediction.

We introduce UP-VLA, a single autoregressive VLA trained with two complementary objectives: (1) multi-modal understanding from large-scale image-text data, and (2) future visual prediction from robot video and action data. They are fused through unified prompting and attention masking, so that action generation can condition on both semantic scene descriptions and predicted visual futures.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item UP-VLA, a unified VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in an autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU tokens and discrete predictive tokens in one LLM.
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism that conditions actions on both semantic scene descriptions and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art results on CALVIN (ABC$\rightarrow$D average length 4.08, +33\% over GR-1) and real-world improvements in both semantic generalization and precision tasks.
\end{itemize}

