\section{Related Works}
\textbf{VLM-based VLAs.} Language-conditioned robot policies have a long history \citep{stepputtis2020language,jang2022bc,shridhar2022cliport,lynch2020grounding}. Recent work builds policies on pre-trained VLMs, e.g., RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt}, OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla}, RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision}, $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0} and 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d}, inheriting semantic generalization from internet-scale data. Such models are pre-trained mainly for high-level understanding, which motivates attempts to inject spatial awareness, e.g., visual trace prompting \citep{zheng2024tracevla} and spatial VQA data \citep{chen2024spatialvlm}.

\textbf{Prediction-based policies.} Another line uses future prediction to ground control in visual dynamics: GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} with video generative pre-training, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} with image-editing diffusion subgoals, UniPi \citep{du2024learning} with text-guided video generation, and joint denoising of images and actions \citep{guo2024prediction}. They gain spatial and physical consistency but lack the semantic robustness of VLMs. 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d} and diffusion policies \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy} are further strong non-VLM baselines.

\textbf{Unified understanding and generation.} Unified multimodal models combine comprehension and generation \citep{dong2023dreamllm,ge2024seed,xie2024show}, building on autoregressive image generation with discrete tokenizers \citep{esser2021taming,sun2024autoregressive}. UP-VLA brings this idea to embodied control by combining understanding and prediction for action generation.

\section{Preliminaries}
We study language-conditioned manipulation: given the current observation $O_t$ and an instruction $L$, the policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. Evaluation on CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} asks an agent to complete five chained tasks in sequence, and we report the success rate at each chain length and the average length of tasks completed in a row.

\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Overview}
UP-VLA is an autoregressive VLA with a single LLM backbone, Phi-1.5 \citep{li2023textbooks}, that performs (i) multi-modal understanding, (ii) future image token prediction and (iii) action generation (\cref{fig:overview}). Images are encoded by two pathways: a continuous CLIP-ViT encoder \citep{radford2021learning} with an MLP projection $E_1$ into the language embedding space, giving image tokens $u$ for MMU, and a discrete VQ-GAN tokenizer $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} giving codebook tokens $v$ for prediction; a VQ decoder reconstructs images. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

