\subsection{Visualization}
\Cref{fig:vis} shows that UP-VLA identifies objects and approximate relative positions, which assists action grounding, though it occasionally misidentifies objects owing to backbone and data scale. Predicted futures show robot and object motion consistent with the instructions, but with background color artifacts in unseen scenes (D versus ABC), suggesting limited generative generalization.

\section{Limitations}
Generative artifacts in prediction (e.g., background color shifts across scenes) indicate insufficient visual generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. Occasional object misclassification in MMU highlights the limits of backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and richer curated data could reduce errors. Joint tasks add training cost and sequence length, which parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory may reduce. Extending to 3D inputs (depth, point clouds), multiple views or explicit physics modeling could further enhance spatial and dynamics understanding.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding with future visual prediction in a single autoregressive VLA. By conditioning action generation on scene descriptions and predicted futures, it attains state-of-the-art results on long-horizon CALVIN (4.08 on ABC$\rightarrow$D, 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D) and transfers to real-world manipulation, improving both semantic generalization and precision. It offers a practical path toward generalist robotic policies with balanced high-level and low-level capabilities.

